@page "/codex"
@using System.Globalization
@inject ArkadeHeroesClient Api

@* The reference: every trait, every rarity band, every number that decides a fight or a birth.
   Values that the server publishes are read LIVE from it, so this page describes the server you are
   actually playing on rather than the one that was compiled. The rest are Core defaults, pinned by
   CodexReferenceTests so a retune fails the build instead of quietly making this page wrong. *@

<PageTitle>Codex · Arkade Heroes</PageTitle>

<div class="page-head">
    <h2>Codex</h2>
    <span class="hud">every trait, every band, every number</span>
    <span class="spacer"></span>
    <a class="btn ghost" href="/">← back to the arena</a>
</div>

<p class="muted codex-intro">
    A hero is one strand and nothing else — every number below is read back out of it. None of this is
    hidden from you, and none of it can be changed after a creature is born.
</p>

@* ---- TRAITS ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- *@
<div class="page-head" style="margin-top:2rem"><h3 style="margin:0">The eight traits</h3>
    <span class="hud">what you see, and what it does</span></div>
<p class="muted cx-note">
    Every hero carries all eight. Each one holds two values: the one it <b>wears</b>, and the one it
    only <b>carries</b> — invisible on this creature, but able to surface in its children.
</p>

<div class="grid cols codex-traits">
    @foreach (var t in TraitRef)
    {
        <div class="card codex-trait">
            <div class="page-head" style="margin:0 0 .4rem">
                <h4 style="margin:0">@t.Name</h4>
                <span class="spacer"></span>
                <span class="chip @(t.IsAffinity ? "ok" : "")">@(t.IsAffinity ? "affinity" : "passive")</span>
            </div>
            <p class="muted" style="margin:0;font-size:.85rem">@t.What</p>
        </div>
    }
</div>
<p class="codex-foot" style="max-width:70ch">
    The six <b>passives</b> shape the creature and, when the server has innate abilities switched on
    (@(_innate is null ? "checking…" : _innate == true ? "on here" : "off here")), buy a chance at a
    combat proc. The two <b>affinities</b> always nudge damage directly — see the table below.
</p>

@* ---- RARITY ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- *@
<div class="page-head" style="margin-top:2.2rem"><h3 style="margin:0">Rarity</h3>
    <span class="hud">how the 256 possible values are carved up</span></div>
<p class="muted cx-note">
    Every trait value is a number from 0 to 255, and where it falls decides its tier. The bands are
    deliberately lopsided: four fifths of the space is Common, and exactly one value in the whole range
    is Legendary.
</p>

<div class="card" style="overflow-x:auto">
    <table class="codex-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Tier</th><th>Values</th><th>Share of the range</th>
                <th>Rarity score</th><th>Affinity bonus</th><th>Sterility risk</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            @foreach (var r in RarityRef)
            {
                <tr>
                    <td><span class="tier-dot @r.Css"></span><b class="@r.Css">@r.Tier</b></td>
                    <td class="mono">@r.Range</td>
                    <td class="mono">@r.Count of 256 · @Pct(r.Count / 256.0 * 100)</td>
                    <td class="mono">@r.Weight</td>
                    <td class="mono">+@Pct(r.Affinity)</td>
                    <td class="mono">@(r.Sterile == 0 ? "—" : $"{r.Sterile}%")</td>
                </tr>
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
<p class="codex-foot" style="max-width:78ch">
    <b>Share of the range</b> is how much of the number space a tier occupies — not your odds of getting
    one. An inherited trait is whatever your parents had; only a fresh mutation rolls against the space.
    <b>Rarity score</b> is what the boards rank by, summed across a hero's eight worn traits (a value of
    exactly 0 is "plain" and scores nothing). <b>Affinity bonus</b> applies only to the two affinity
    traits, and all bonuses together are capped at +@Pct(AffinityCapPct) — an affinity nudges a fight, it
    never decides one. <b>Sterility risk</b> is the chance a hero of that tier is born unable to breed:
    the price of chasing rarity.
</p>

@* ---- MUTATION -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *@
<div class="page-head" style="margin-top:2.2rem"><h3 style="margin:0">Mutation</h3>
    <span class="hud">when a trait comes back new</span></div>
<p class="muted cx-note">
    About one trait in forty-three comes out of a breeding as something neither parent carried. When that
    happens, this is what it rolls — weighted hard toward the bottom, so a Legendary mutation stays a
    story rather than a Tuesday.
</p>
<div class="card">
    <div class="codex-odds">
        @foreach (var m in MutationOdds)
        {
            <span class="codex-odd @m.Css" style="flex:@F(m.Share)" title="@m.Tier @Pct(m.Share)"></span>
        }
    </div>
    <div class="codex-odd-keys">
        @foreach (var m in MutationOdds)
        {
            <span class="@m.Css"><i></i>@m.Tier <b>@Pct(m.Share)</b></span>
        }
    </div>
</div>

@* ---- ELEMENTS -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *@
<div class="page-head" style="margin-top:2.2rem"><h3 style="margin:0">Elements</h3>
    <span class="hud">a ring of eight — each beats the next</span></div>
<p class="muted cx-note">
    Every hero has exactly one element. There is no best one: each is strong against the element after it
    and weak against the one before, all the way around the circle.
</p>
<div class="grid cols codex-elements">
    @foreach (var (name, index) in Elements.Select((e, n) => (e, n)))
    {
        <div class="card codex-element">
            <div class="hud">@name</div>
            <div class="cx-el-line ok">beats @Elements[(index + 1) % Elements.Length]</div>
            <div class="cx-el-line bad">loses to @Elements[(index + Elements.Length - 1) % Elements.Length]</div>
        </div>
    }
</div>
<p class="codex-foot">
    A favourable matchup multiplies damage by <b class="mono">@F(ElementStrong)×</b>; an unfavourable one
    by <b class="mono">@F(ElementWeak)×</b>. A critical hit multiplies by
    <b class="mono">@F(CritMultiplier)×</b>.
</p>

@* ---- BUILD ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- *@
<div class="page-head" style="margin-top:2.2rem"><h3 style="margin:0">The rest of the strand</h3>
    <span class="hud">what never shows on the outside</span></div>
<div class="grid cols">
    <div class="card">
        <h4 style="margin:0 0 .5rem">Stats</h4>
        <p class="muted" style="margin:0;font-size:.86rem">
            <b>Might</b> drives physical damage, <b>Vitality</b> health, <b>Speed</b> who moves first,
            <b>Cunning</b> magical damage, and <b>Fortune</b> the chance of a critical hit. Each starts
            from the strand and grows as the hero levels.
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
        <h4 style="margin:0 0 .5rem">Hidden growth</h4>
        <p class="muted" style="margin:0;font-size:.86rem">
            A second, invisible number per stat that decides how fast it grows. Two heroes with identical
            starting stats can end up nothing alike by level 20 — which is why a low-level hero is a bet,
            not a known quantity.
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
        <h4 style="margin:0 0 .5rem">Techniques</h4>
        <p class="muted" style="margin:0;font-size:.86rem">
            Two technique slots drawn from the strand. The first is known from level 1, the second unlocks
            at level @GeneSkillBLevel, and the Elemental Burst at level @BurstLevel.
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
        <h4 style="margin:0 0 .5rem">Recovery</h4>
        <p class="muted" style="margin:0;font-size:.86rem">
            How long this hero needs between breedings. Inherited like everything else — and worth
            checking before you plan a bloodline around one creature.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<p class="codex-note muted" style="margin-top:2rem">
    Numbers marked live are read from this server as you load the page; the rest are the game's defaults
    and are checked against the code on every build. If an operator retunes their server, the fight
    outcomes it publishes carry the rules version they were resolved under, so an old replay stays
    checkable under the rules it actually ran on.
</p>

@code {
    // Live from the server where it publishes the value — this page should describe the server you are
    // on, not the one that happened to be compiled.
    private bool? _innate;

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        try { _innate = (await Api.Chain.InfoAsync()).Config?.InnateAbilities; }
        catch { _innate = null; }
    }

    static string F(double v) => v.ToString("0.##", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);
    static string Pct(double v) => v.ToString("0.#", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture) + "%";

    record Trait(string Name, string What, bool IsAffinity);

    static readonly Trait[] TraitRef =
    [
        new("Aura", "The glow a creature carries. The first thing you notice across a roster.", false),
        new("Markings", "Patterning over the body — stripes, spots, scars, plating.", false),
        new("Eyes", "Eye form and colour, and the single most recognisable feature at a glance.", false),
        new("Crest", "What it wears on its head: horns, a crown, a fin, nothing at all.", false),
        new("Sigil", "A mark it was born with. Rare sigils are the collector's chase.", false),
        new("Stance", "How it holds itself — and how it reads in a fight before a blow lands.", false),
        new("Element affinity", "How well it channels its own element. Adds damage directly.", true),
        new("Temperament", "Its disposition in a fight. Adds damage directly, like the affinity above.", true),
    ];

    record Band(string Tier, string Css, string Range, int Count, int Weight, double Affinity, int Sterile);

    // RarityBands.Default(255, 253, 241, 206, weights 50/20/8/3/1), AffinityBonuses.Default and
    // SterilityChances.Default(50, 30, 15, 5). Pinned by CodexReferenceTests.
    static readonly Band[] RarityRef =
    [
        new("Legendary", "legendary", "255",     1,   50, 3.0, 50),
        new("Epic",      "epic",      "253–254", 2,   20, 2.0, 30),
        new("Rare",      "rare",      "241–252", 12,   8, 1.2, 15),
        new("Uncommon",  "uncommon",  "206–240", 35,   3, 0.6,  5),
        new("Common",    "common",    "0–205",   206,  1, 0.2,  0),
    ];

    const double AffinityCapPct = 5.0;
    const double ElementStrong = 1.3, ElementWeak = 0.75, CritMultiplier = 1.5;
    const int GeneSkillBLevel = 6, BurstLevel = 9;

    record Odd(string Tier, string Css, double Share);

    // GeneMixer.MutatedVariant's cutoffs: 176 / 56 / 18 / 4 / 2 out of 256.
    static readonly Odd[] MutationOdds =
    [
        new("Common", "common", 176 / 256d * 100),
        new("Uncommon", "uncommon", 56 / 256d * 100),
        new("Rare", "rare", 18 / 256d * 100),
        new("Epic", "epic", 4 / 256d * 100),
        new("Legendary", "legendary", 2 / 256d * 100),
    ];

    // Element enum order — each is strong against the NEXT in this ring.
    static readonly string[] Elements =
        ["Ember", "Gale", "Terra", "Tide", "Volt", "Frost", "Radiant", "Umbral"];
}
